<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ferdinand I of Naples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1424">1424</a>–<a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ferrante of Naples</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ferdinand I, known as Ferrante, was King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> from 1458 to 1494, the illegitimate son of Alfonso V of Aragon. He defeated a baronial revolt backed by the Angevin claimant early in his reign and crushed the Conspiracy of the Barons in 1485 to 1486. His shrewd and ruthless rule made Naples a major Italian power and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> learning.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples.jpg" alt="Ferdinand I of Naples" width="320" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ferdinando_I_Napoli.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
